{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "\"To access the print version of the article, a link is provided. The rules for solving the Sudoku puzzle are similar to the standard rules, with a special condition applied to the cells contained within grey lines. These cells must add up to the figures indicated in the corner, and each number can only be used once within each shape formed by the grey lines.\"\n\n\n \nAddition to the standard Sudoku rules, the grey lines form unique shapes that require the numbers inside them to sum up to the values shown in the corners. The puzzle requires careful planning and attention to detail to ensure that each shape contains the correct numbers without any duplicates. The rules are designed to challenge and engage the solver, making it a fun and rewarding experience.\n\n\n \nThe Sudoku puzzle is a popular and entertaining activity that can be enjoyed by people of all ages and skill levels. The rules are easy to understand, but the puzzle itself can be quite challenging, requiring a combination of logic and problem-solving skills. The use of grey lines to form unique shapes adds an extra layer of complexity and interest to the puzzle.\n\n\n \nThe completed puzzle will be available in next week's Observer Digital Edition, allowing solvers to check their work and see the solution. This provides an opportunity to learn from any mistakes and improve puzzle-solving skills for the future. The Observer Digital Edition is a great resource for puzzle enthusiasts, offering a wide range of challenging and engaging activities.\n\n\n \nThe Sudoku puzzle is a great way to challenge the mind and have fun at the same time. With its unique rules and shapes, it offers a refreshing twist on the classic puzzle. Whether you are a seasoned puzzle solver or just starting out, the Sudoku puzzle is a gr